---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Editorial Policy — How devsyllabus Writes, Reviews, and Updates Content"
  description="devsyllabus editorial policy: how content is written, reviewed, fact-checked, updated, and corrected, and the standards every page must meet."
  path="/editorial-policy/"
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Editorial Policy' }]} />

  <div class="doc">
  <h1>Editorial policy</h1>
  <p>
    This page describes how content on devsyllabus is created, reviewed,
    and maintained. It applies to every study note, article, and practice
    question on the site.
  </p>

  <h2>Who writes content</h2>
  <p>
    Every page carries a named author. Authors' backgrounds, areas of
    expertise, and credentials are published on their
    <a href="/authors/">author pages</a>. We do not publish unattributed
    or ghost-written content.
  </p>

  <h2>How content is reviewed</h2>
  <p>
    Before publication, every technical page is reviewed by a second team
    member who did not write it. The reviewer checks:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Technical accuracy of every claim, example, and code snippet.</li>
    <li>Consistency with the primary sources cited in the references.</li>
    <li>That practice question answers and explanations agree.</li>
    <li>That the page delivers what its title and description promise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fact-checking and sources</h2>
  <p>
    Technical content is grounded in primary sources: RFCs for HTTP and
    REST, standard textbooks for database theory and programming, and
    official documentation for tooling. Each note lists its sources in a
    references section. Where a standard is contested or commonly
    mis-taught, the note says so explicitly rather than presenting one
    convention as universal.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>
    Pages show their original publication date and last-updated date.
    Material is reviewed when the underlying standard changes (for
    example, an RFC revision), when we receive a correction, or as part of
    a periodic review cycle. Significant corrections are noted on the
    page.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Found an error? <a href="/contact/">Tell us</a>. Verified corrections
    are applied promptly, and substantive changes to a page's technical
    claims are reflected in its updated date.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      We do not publish machine-generated bulk content. Every page is
      written by a person on our team.
    </li>
    <li>
      We do not write for search engines: no keyword stuffing, no thin
      pages that exist only to rank, no duplicated content across URLs.
    </li>
    <li>
      We do not accept paid placements in content. Pages either provide
      genuine value or they do not exist.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Related policies: <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a>,
    <a href="/fact-checking/">fact-checking process</a>,
    <a href="/update-log/">update log</a>, and
    <a href="/sources/">how we cite sources</a>.
  </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
